    body_en: "Credit scores were originally a financial metric devised to predict a borrower's ability to repay a loan. But their range of use now goes far beyond loan screening. The domains in which credit information intervenes have steadily expanded to include lease contracts, hiring reviews, and even insurance enrollment. The problem is that credit scores are easily mistaken for an indicator of a person's overall trustworthiness. Even though in reality it is only a prediction based on the narrow data of past repayment history, a low score is often treated as if it were evidence of a personal character flaw. A more serious problem is the vicious cycle in which a falling credit score produces yet another disadvantage. It is a structure in which a low score leads to loan rejection, and a person who cannot raise emergency funds after being rejected falls into delinquency again. This cycle appears more strongly among those who were economically vulnerable to begin with. As algorithms become involved in calculating scores, the opacity of the basis for that judgment has also become a new concern. It is not uncommon for the person concerned to be unable to even find out why their score fell. In the end, the credit information system has reached a point where it must consider not only the accuracy of prediction but also responsibility for the social consequences that information produces.",
