    body_en: "The right to counsel in the criminal justice system is regarded as the foundation of the process for determining guilt or innocence. Whether that right is substantively realized, however, is a separate question. The system by which the state assigns counsel to a defendant unable to afford a private attorney was established to fill this gap. The problem is that the caseload a single public defender must handle is excessively large. A defender who must process dozens of cases within limited time struggles to find room for sufficient investigation and preparation for any individual case. As a result, a gap in the substantive quality of defense between defendants with financial means and those without repeats itself. This is a point that directly conflicts with the principle of equality before the law. In some regions, attempts have been made to restructure public defender compensation to be proportional to time required rather than number of cases. A proposal to entrust the authority for assigning defenders to an independent body rather than the courts, in order to guarantee the defender's independence, is also under discussion. In the end, reforming the public defense system is both a matter of protecting the fairness of criminal procedure and a matter of shoring up the foundation of trust in the justice system as a whole.",
